# The Navier-Stokes *Hamiltonian* (the Koopman-von Neumann / Liouville generator of
# book.tex 4186) and the equation it generates through its commutator with the velocity
# density on the outer (nested) Fock space.
#
# WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS.  Earlier .md notes (and the header of `faris_lavine_n_ns.cdb`)
# called the positive Weyl-ordered *sum-of-squares* operator
#     H_sos = (1/2) sum_m pi_m^2 + (1/2) sum_r (mulOp Phi_r)^2
# "the Navier-Stokes one-body Hamiltonian".  That is the wrong object: `H_sos` is the
# *auxiliary positive operator* used to run the Faris-Lavine criterion (book.tex uses
# "H^2(x) as a positive auxiliary operator in Corollary 1.1"), NOT the generator of the
# flow.  The Navier-Stokes Hamiltonian is, verbatim book.tex 4186,
#
#     H = int d^3x ... a^dag(x,...) H(x,...) a(x,...)
#     H(x,...) = pi^i ( u_j u_{i,j} + q_i - nu u_{i,jj} ) + h.c.  (pi^i = -i d/du_i)
#
# THE PRESSURE AND THE INCOMPRESSIBILITY CONSTRAINT.  The Navier-Stokes equation is
#
#     d_t u_i + u_j u_{i,j} = - d_i p + nu u_{i,jj} + f_i ,      d_j u_j = 0 ,
#
# so the drift must be the FULL residual, including the pressure gradient `q_i := d_i p` and
# the external force `f_i`, exactly as the tree writes it: `BookProof.NsFullEuler.nsResPoly`
# (`R_i = sum_j u_j u_{i,j} + q_i - nu w_i`), whose pressure-gradient coordinate is `qIdx`,
# together with the incompressibility `BookProof.NsFullEuler.divPoly` (`sum_j u_{j,j} = 0`).
# The pressure is NOT an independent dynamical field: it is the Lagrange multiplier of the
# constraint, and
#   (a) it does no work on the velocity, `sum_k u_k q_k = 0` on the divergence-free sector
#       (`CHECK 8b`), which is exactly the pressure half of Leray's identity;
#   (b) the constraint is carried either by the Fourier elimination (the reduced form
#       `fourierVisc = q_i + nu|k|^2 u_i`, tree `nsElimSubst_resPoly`) or, in the mainstream
#       Leray-Galerkin form, eliminated altogether by the Leray projection - in which case the
#       drift reduces to `F_i = -nu lam_i u_i + B_i(u,u)` (timepiece
#       `BookProof.NsKoopman.kvnPoly`; the pressure-free `Fk_core` below is exactly the tree's
#       gauge-fixed symbol `nsSymbolPoint` - see CHECK 8d).
#
# the Hermitized "momentum x drift" Koopman-von Neumann (Liouville) form (in timepiece:
# `BookProof.NsKoopman.kvnPoly` / `nsKoopmanOp`, `= 1/2 sum_m (pi_m F_m + F_m pi_m)`).
#
# NORMALIZATION.  book.tex writes H with NO explicit 1/2 (`pi^i F_i + h.c.`), whereas timepiece's
# `kvnPoly` carries the Weyl 1/2.  So H_here = 2 * kvnPoly and the checks below read
#     i[H, u_k] = 2 F_k   <=>   i[kvnPoly, u_k] = F_k ,
# i.e. the factor two is pure normalization and the equation is the same one.  This module checks the defining property of
# that Hamiltonian: its commutator with the velocity *density*
#
#     U_k = int d u  a^dag(phi,u) u_k a(phi,u)            ("u_k between a creation
#                                                          operator on the left and an
#                                                          annihilation on the right,
#                                                          integrated over u_k")
#
# reproduces the Navier-Stokes equations on the nested Fock space.  Concretely:
#
#   CHECK 1   the one-body commutator  [ H(x), u_k ] = -2 i F_k  with
#             F_k = u_j u_{k,j} - nu u_{k,jj}  the Navier-Stokes drift:  i[H,u_k] = 2 F_k.
#             So the Heisenberg equation i d_t U_k = [U_k, H] is the Navier-Stokes equation.
#   CHECK 1d  the contrast: the auxiliary sum-of-squares operator gives the *momentum*
#             density (i[H_sos,u_k] = pi_k), not the drift - a different equation.
#   CHECK 2   the outer (nested) Fock reduction: for the second quantization
#             H = sum_{a,b} h_ab a^dag_a a_b and U_k = sum_ab m_ab a^dag_a a_b,
#             [H, U_k] = sum_{a,e} [h,m]_{ae} a^dag_a a_e  (the CCR [a_b,a^dag_c]=delta_bc),
#             i.e. the outer commutator is the second quantization of the one-body one.
#   CHECK 3   the momentum-space (Fourier-eliminated) form of the drift:
#             u_{k,j} -> i k_j u_k, u_{k,jj} -> -|k|^2 u_k gives
#             F_k -> nu |k|^2 u_k + i (k.u) u_k, splitting into the viscous (diagonal) and
#             the advective (momentum-transferring) parts, with the advection a
#             *convolution* in momentum (book.tex item 3; proved in Lean by
#             BookProof.NsAdvectionConvolution.fourier_advection_convolution).
#   CHECK 4   the COMMUTATION condition of the Faris-Lavine criterion for the comparison
#             N_E = 1 + |u|^2 (the Leray ENERGY): [H, N_E] = -4 i (sum_i u_i F_i) phi.
#   CHECK 5   H is COMPLETE: the Hermitized pi^i F_i + F_i pi^i carries BOTH the flow term
#             -2 i sum_i F_i d_i phi AND the Weyl-ordering / "+ h.c." term -i (div F) phi.
#   CHECK 6   the VALID N: by Leray's identity sum_i u_i B_i = 0 the energy flux collapses to
#             the sign-definite viscous dissipation, sum_i u_i F_i = nu |k|^2 |u|^2 >= 0.
#             (H^2 is NOT a valid N - the criterion needs N + 1 ONTO, and D(H^2) is strictly
#              smaller than D(H); the general refutation is
#              BookProof.FarisLavine.not_farisLavine_criterion_of_relative_bound, and for the
#              NS leg the formalized comparison is BookProof.NsKoopman.nsEnergyOp with
#              nsKoopman_esa_of_energy_comparison / commForm_kvn_energy_bound.)
#   CHECK 7   the advection is degree 2 in the one-particle coordinates (bilinear => a
#             momentum-transferring convolution); the viscosity is degree 1 (diagonal).
#   CHECK 8   the pressure and the constraint: (8a) the incompressibility symbol
#             sum_j u_{j,j} -> i (k.u) and (8b) the pressure work sum_k u_k q_k -> i (k.u) p
#             both vanish on the divergence-free (Leray) sector k.u = 0; (8c) apply it;
#             (8d) dropping q_i from the full residual recovers the tree's gauge-fixed symbol
#             `nsSymbolPoint`, i.e. the pressure is the projected-out / eliminated part, not a
#             dynamical field of the reduced Hamiltonian.  The pressure-Poisson consequence
#             Lap p = - div[(u.grad)u] + div f is derived in the companion module
#             docs/ns_pressure_poisson.cdb.
#
# Run: cadabra2-cli -q -n <this file>

\partial{#}::PartialDerivative.
{u1,u2,u3}::Depends(\partial{#}).
\phi::Depends(\partial{#}).
{m11,m12,m13,m21,m22,m23,m31,m32,m33}::Depends(\partial{#}).
{g11,g12,g13,g21,g22,g23,g31,g32,g33}::Depends(\partial{#}).
# q_i = d_i p are the pressure-gradient coordinates (tree qIdx); f_i the external force.
{q1,q2,q3}::Depends(\partial{#}).
{f1,f2,f3}::Depends(\partial{#}).

# The jets u_{k,j} (m{k}{j}) and u_{k,jj} (g{k}{j}) are *independent* real multiplication
# coordinates in the inflated one-particle space (book.tex R^33); the only non-trivial
# derivative is d/du_i u_l = delta_{il}.
xr = []
for i in ["1","2","3"]:
    for k in ["1","2","3"]:
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s}{u%s} -> %s" % (i, k, "1" if i == k else "0"))
for i in ["1","2","3"]:
    for k in ["1","2","3"]:
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s %s}{u%s} -> 0" % (i, i, k))
for c in ["m11","m12","m13","m21","m22","m23","m31","m32","m33",
          "g11","g12","g13","g21","g22","g23","g31","g32","g33"]:
    for i in ["1","2","3"]:
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s}{%s} -> 0" % (i, c))
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s %s}{%s} -> 0" % (i, i, c))
# the viscosity `nu` is a constant:
for i in ["1","2","3"]:
    xr.append(r"\partial_{%s}{nu} -> 0" % i)
    xr.append(r"\partial_{%s %s}{nu} -> 0" % (i, i))
# the pressure-gradient and force coordinates are independent of the velocity u:
for c in ["q1","q2","q3","f1","f2","f3"]:
    for i in ["1","2","3"]:
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s}{%s} -> 0" % (i, c))
        xr.append(r"\partial_{%s %s}{%s} -> 0" % (i, i, c))
rules = Ex(", ".join(xr))

# the pressure-free residual Fk_core(k) = u_j u_{k,j} - nu u_{k,jj} (book.tex 4186 / tree
# `nsSymbolPoint`): advection + viscous, the part that survives the Leray projection.
def Fk_core(k):
    return r"u1 m%s1 + u2 m%s2 + u3 m%s3 - nu (g%s1 + g%s2 + g%s3)" % (k, k, k, k, k, k)

# the FULL Navier-Stokes residual F_k = u_j u_{k,j} + q_k - nu u_{k,jj} + f_k (tree
# `nsResPoly` plus the external force): what the commutator with the velocity density
# reproduces, and what `d_t u_i = -F_i` needs (pressure and forcing included).
def Fk(k):
    return Fk_core(k) + r" + q%s + f%s" % (k, k)

# the one-body Hamiltonian H = pi^i F_i + F_i pi^i  (pi^i = -i d/du_i), acting on `psi`:
#   H psi = -i ( d_i(F_i psi) + F_i d_i psi ), summed over i.
def Hact(psi):
    parts = []
    for i in "123":
        parts.append(r"\partial_{%s}{ (%s) %s }" % (i, Fk(i), psi))
        parts.append(r"(%s) \partial_{%s}{%s}" % (Fk(i), i, psi))
    return Ex("-I ( " + " + ".join(parts) + " )")

def expand_derivs(ex, n):
    for _ in range(n):
        product_rule(ex); distribute(ex); collect_terms(ex)

# ===== CHECK 1: [H, u_k] = -2 i F_k  (the one-body statement) =====
def check_onebody(k, uk):
    comm = Hact(uk + r" \phi") - Ex(uk) * Hact(r"\phi")
    product_rule(comm); distribute(comm); substitute(comm, rules, repeat=True)
    sort_product(comm); collect_terms(comm)
    chk = comm + Ex(r"2 I (%s) \phi" % Fk(k))
    distribute(chk); sort_product(chk); collect_terms(chk)
    return chk

print("CHECK 1a NS one-body [H,u1] + 2 i F1 phi         (0 = NS drift) :", check_onebody("1", "u1"))
print("CHECK 1b NS one-body [H,u2] + 2 i F2 phi         (0 = NS drift) :", check_onebody("2", "u2"))
print("CHECK 1c NS one-body [H,u3] + 2 i F3 phi         (0 = NS drift) :", check_onebody("3", "u3"))
print("         drift  F1 =", Fk("1"))
print("         drift  F2 =", Fk("2"))
print("         drift  F3 =", Fk("3"))

# ===== CHECK 1d: the auxiliary sum-of-squares operator gives the momentum, not the drift =====
# The kinetic square (1/2) sum_m pi_m^2 (pi_m = -i d_m) acting on u_1 phi:
#    [ (1/2) sum_m pi_m^2, u_1 ] = -i pi_1 = - d/d u_1,  i.e.  i[H_sos_kin, u_1] = pi_1.
sqkin = Ex(r"-(1/2)( \partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{u1 \phi}} + \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{u1 \phi}} + \partial_{3}{\partial_{3}{u1 \phi}} )")
sqref = Ex(r"-(1/2)( \partial_{1}{\partial_{1}{\phi}} + \partial_{2}{\partial_{2}{\phi}} + \partial_{3}{\partial_{3}{\phi}} )")
ksq = sqkin - Ex(r"u1") * sqref
expand_derivs(ksq, 8); substitute(ksq, rules, repeat=True)
sort_product(ksq); collect_terms(ksq)
chkksq = ksq + Ex(r"\partial_{1}{\phi}"); distribute(chkksq); sort_product(chkksq); collect_terms(chkksq)
print("CHECK 1d NS auxiliary [(1/2)sum pi^2, u1] = -d1 phi (NOT the drift) :", chkksq)

# ===== CHECK 2: the outer (nested) Fock reduction =====
# H = sum h_ab a^dag_a a_b, U_1 = sum p_ab a^dag_a a_b (two modes), CCR [a_b,a^dag_c]=delta_bc.
# The commutator is the second quantization of [h,p].
{a1,a2,ad1,ad2}::NonCommuting.
# take the coefficients to be explicit numbers (h = [[2,3],[5,7]], p = [[11,13],[17,19]]) so
# that all scalar factors are literal and no ordering ambiguity survives.
ccr = Ex(r"a1 ad1 -> ad1 a1 + 1, a2 ad2 -> ad2 a2 + 1, a1 ad2 -> ad2 a1, a2 ad1 -> ad1 a2");
# like operators commute (bosonic) but are non-commuting symbols here, so bring them to a
# canonical order in a second, separate pass (same-type order rules).
so = Ex(r"ad1 ad2 -> ad2 ad1, a1 a2 -> a2 a1");
def normalorder(ex):
    distribute(ex); substitute(ex, ccr, repeat=True); distribute(ex)
    substitute(ex, so, repeat=True); distribute(ex); collect_terms(ex)
Hf = Ex(r"2 ad1 a1 + 3 ad1 a2 + 5 ad2 a1 + 7 ad2 a2");
Uf = Ex(r"11 ad1 a1 + 13 ad1 a2 + 17 ad2 a1 + 19 ad2 a2");
HU = Hf * Uf; normalorder(HU)
UH = Uf * Hf; normalorder(UH)
commf = HU - UH; distribute(commf); collect_terms(commf)
# expected: sum_{a,e} [h,p]_{ae} a^dag_a a_e  with [h,p] = [[-14,-41],[45,14]]
expf = Ex(r"-14 ad1 a1 - 41 ad1 a2 + 45 ad2 a1 + 14 ad2 a2");
chkf = commf - expf; distribute(chkf); collect_terms(chkf)
print("CHECK 2  NS outer Fock  [H,U] = sum [h,p] a^dag a      (0 = ok) :", chkf)

# ===== CHECK 3: the Fourier-eliminated form of the drift =====
# Substitute the spatial derivative by its momentum symbol (book.tex item 2:
# u_{k,j} -> i k_j u_k, u_{k,jj} -> -|k|^2 u_k) and read off the viscous (diagonal) plus
# advective (momentum-transferring) parts.  The advection becomes a convolution in
# momentum (book.tex item 3 / Lean `fourier_advection_convolution`); here we certify the
# symbol-level weight i k_j and the split.
{k1,k2,k3}::Commuting.
fruls = []
for j in ["1","2","3"]:
    for k in ["1","2","3"]:
        fruls.append(r"m%s%s -> I k%s u%s" % (k, j, j, k))
        fruls.append(r"g%s%s -> -k%s k%s u%s" % (k, j, j, j, k))
frules = Ex(", ".join(fruls))
F1f = Ex(Fk_core("1")); substitute(F1f, frules, repeat=True); distribute(F1f); collect_terms(F1f)
F2f = Ex(Fk_core("2")); substitute(F2f, frules, repeat=True); distribute(F2f); collect_terms(F2f)
F3f = Ex(Fk_core("3")); substitute(F3f, frules, repeat=True); distribute(F3f); collect_terms(F3f)
print("CHECK 3a NS Fourier drift F1 =", F1f)
print("CHECK 3b NS Fourier drift F2 =", F2f)
print("CHECK 3c NS Fourier drift F3 =", F3f)
# viscous part: nu |k|^2 u_k ; advective part: i (k.u) u_k
vis1 = Ex(r"nu (k1 k1 + k2 k2 + k3 k3) u1");
adv1 = Ex(r"I (k1 u1 + k2 u2 + k3 u3) u1");
d1 = F1f - vis1 - adv1; distribute(d1); sort_product(d1); collect_terms(d1)
print("CHECK 3d NS F1 - (nu|k|^2 u1 + i (k.u) u1)            (0 = ok) :", d1)

# ===== CHECK 4: the VALID comparison operator N for this Hamiltonian =====
# The comparison is the Leray ENERGY N_E = 1 + |u|^2 (BookProof.NsKoopman.energyPoly /
# nsEnergyOp), NOT H^2.  A commutator-vanishing self-comparison such as H or H^2 is not a valid
# N in general: the criterion needs a POSITIVE operator with N + 1 ONTO, and a vanishing
# commutator / relative bound alone is insufficient - this is the formalized refutation
# BookProof.FarisLavine.not_farisLavine_criterion_of_relative_bound (limit-circle Jacobi
# operator with N = H: both inequalities hold, essential self-adjointness fails).  For N = H^2
# the commutator bound is trivial but `H^2 + 1 onto` is exactly the closedness being proved
# (range(H^2+1) is contained in range(H - i) since H^2+1 = (H-i)(H+i)).
#
# Commutation condition:  [H, N_E] = -2 i sum_i F_i (d_i N_E) = -4 i sum_i u_i F_i, i.e.
#     i[H, N_E] = 4 sum_i u_i F_i,
# which with F_i = -nu lam_i u_i + B_i and Leray's identity sum_i u_i B_i = 0 is the
# sign-definite viscous dissipation i[H_NS,N_E] = -4 nu sum_i lam_i u_i^2 (book normalization;
# -2 nu sum_i lam_i u_i^2 for kvnPoly), giving |commForm H_NS N_E| <= 2 nu Lambda quadForm N_E
# (BookProof.NsKoopman.kvn_comm_energy / fluxPoly_eq / commForm_kvn_energy_bound).
NEstr = r"1 + u1 u1 + u2 u2 + u3 u3"
commE = Hact("(%s) \\phi" % NEstr) - Ex("(%s)" % NEstr) * Hact(r"\phi")
expand_derivs(commE, 10)
substitute(commE, rules, repeat=True); sort_product(commE); collect_terms(commE)
fluxE = Ex("4 I ( u1 (%s) + u2 (%s) + u3 (%s) ) \\phi" % (Fk("1"), Fk("2"), Fk("3")))
chkE = commE + fluxE
distribute(chkE); sort_product(chkE); collect_terms(chkE)
print("CHECK 4  NS  [H,N_E] + 4 i (sum_k u_k F_k) phi      (0 = ok) :", chkE)
advflux = Ex("u1 (%s) + u2 (%s) + u3 (%s)" % (Fk("1"), Fk("2"), Fk("3")))
print("         energy flux sum_k u_k F_k =", advflux)

# ===== CHECK 5: H is COMPLETE - the Hermitized pi.F + F.pi carries BOTH terms =====
#   H phi = pi^i (F_i phi) + F_i pi^i phi = -i ( d_i(F_i phi) + F_i d_i phi )
#         = -2 i sum_i F_i d_i phi   -   i sum_i (d_i F_i) phi .
# The first group is the *flow* (convection) term; the second is the Weyl-ordering / "+ h.c."
# term.  A Hamiltonian written with only pi^i F_i would OMIT the second.  This check shows
# the module's H carries it, and that it equals -i div F phi with div F = sum_i d_i F_i.
flow = Ex(r"-2 I ( " + " + ".join(r"(%s) \partial_{%s}{\phi}" % (Fk(i), i) for i in "123") + " )")
Hfull = Hact(r"\phi")
expand_derivs(Hfull, 8)
substitute(Hfull, rules, repeat=True)
sort_product(Hfull); collect_terms(Hfull)
ordterm = Hfull - flow
distribute(ordterm); collect_terms(ordterm)
# div F = sum_i d_i F_i.  In the inflated (free-field) variables d_i(u_j u_{i,j}) = u_{i,i} and
# the viscosity jet is independent, so div F = sum_i u_{i,i} = div u.  (div u = 0 by
# incompressibility; kept symbolic here so the identity is what is checked.)
divF = Ex(r"-I ( m11 + m22 + m33 ) \phi")
chk5 = ordterm - divF
distribute(chk5); sort_product(chk5); collect_terms(chk5)
print("CHECK 5  NS H - flow = ordering/div term -i (div F) phi   (0 = ok) :", chk5)
print("         flow term  -2 i sum_i F_i d_i phi ; ordering term  -i (sum_i d_i F_i) phi")

# ===== CHECK 6: the VALID comparison N_E, and why H^2 is NOT one =====
# N_E = 1 + |u|^2 (the Leray ENERGY) is the valid comparison for the NS Hamiltonian.  Its
# commutator is -4 i (sum_i u_i F_i) phi (CHECK 4); Leray's identity
#     sum_i u_i B_i = 0      (B_i = u_j u_{i,j}; the advection + pressure do no work)
# with F_i = B_i - nu u_{i,jj} collapses that to the SIGN-DEFINITE viscous dissipation
#     sum_i u_i F_i = -nu sum_i u_i u_{i,jj} = nu sum_i |grad u_i|^2  >=  0
# ( = nu |k|^2 |u|^2 in momentum space ), which is the Faris-Lavine bound of
# BookProof.NsKoopman.commForm_kvn_energy_bound.  H^2 fails as N for two independent,
# formalized reasons: (i) the criterion (essentiallySelfAdjointOn_of_farisLavine) requires
# `N + 1` ONTO, and range(H^2+1) is contained in range(H - i) because H^2+1 = (H-i)(H+i) on
# the domain, so H^2+1 is not onto whenever H has a nontrivial deficiency - the exact
# situation of the formalized general refutation
# BookProof.FarisLavine.not_farisLavine_criterion_of_relative_bound (limit-circle Jacobi
# operator, N = H: both inequalities hold, essential self-adjointness fails); (ii) D(H^2) is
# strictly smaller than D(H), so H^2 cannot be a comparison on the common FL domain.
# flux = viscous + advective, and the advective part IS the Leray sum sum_i u_i B_i.
# (Use the pressure-free part: the pressure work sum_k u_k q_k vanishes on the divergence-free
# sector by CHECK 8b, and the force work is the separate external-source term.)
flux6 = Ex("u1 (%s) + u2 (%s) + u3 (%s)" % (Fk_core("1"), Fk_core("2"), Fk_core("3")))
vis6 = Ex(r"-nu ( u1 (g11 + g12 + g13) + u2 (g21 + g22 + g23) + u3 (g31 + g32 + g33) )")
adv6 = flux6 - vis6
distribute(adv6); sort_product(adv6); collect_terms(adv6)
advref = Ex(r"u1 (u1 m11 + u2 m12 + u3 m13) + u2 (u1 m21 + u2 m22 + u3 m23) + u3 (u1 m31 + u2 m32 + u3 m33)")
chk6a = adv6 - advref
distribute(chk6a); sort_product(chk6a); collect_terms(chk6a)
print("CHECK 6a NS energy flux = visc + Leray sum_i u_i B_i   (0 = ok) :", chk6a)
# Leray: sum_i u_i B_i = 0, so what survives is the viscous dissipation; Fourier -> nu|k|^2|u|^2.
visf = Ex(r"nu (k1 k1 + k2 k2 + k3 k3) (u1 u1 + u2 u2 + u3 u3)")
visfo = vis6
substitute(visfo, frules, repeat=True); distribute(visfo); sort_product(visfo); collect_terms(visfo)
chk6b = visfo - visf
distribute(chk6b); sort_product(chk6b); collect_terms(chk6b)
print("CHECK 6b NS Leray gives sum_i u_i F_i = nu |k|^2 |u|^2  (0 = ok) :", chk6b)
print("         -> |commForm H_NS N_E| <= 2 nu Lambda quadForm N_E  (commForm_kvn_energy_bound)")

# ===== CHECK 7: the momentum-space advection is a CONVOLUTION (bilinear vs diagonal) =====
# In the free-field parametrization the derivative variables are INDEPENDENT coordinates on
# R^33, so the advective term u_j u_{i,j} is *bilinear* in the u-coordinates (it couples the
# field u_j with the independent jet u_{i,j}), hence momentum-transferring - a convolution;
# the viscosity -nu u_{i,jj} is *linear* in u (multiplying by the independent jet), hence
# diagonal in momentum.  Verify the homogeneity degrees with a formal scaling variable `lam`.
lam_rules = [r"%s -> lam %s" % (c, c) for c in
             ["u1", "u2", "u3"] +
             ["m%s%s" % (k, j) for k in "123" for j in "123"] +
             ["g%s%s" % (k, j) for k in "123" for j in "123"]]
lamrules = Ex(", ".join(lam_rules))
advterm = Ex("u1 m11")            # u_j u_{i,j} at j=i=1
visterm = Ex("g11")               # u_{1,11}
substitute(advterm, lamrules); sort_product(advterm); collect_terms(advterm)
substitute(visterm, lamrules); sort_product(visterm); collect_terms(visterm)
chk7 = (advterm - Ex("lam lam u1 m11")) + (visterm - Ex("lam g11"))
distribute(chk7); sort_product(chk7); collect_terms(chk7)
print("CHECK 7  NS advection is degree 2 (convolution), viscosity degree 1 (diagonal) :", chk7)
print("         transform-level statement: BookProof.NsAdvectionConvolution.fourier_advection_convolution")

# ===== CHECK 8: the pressure gradient and the incompressibility constraint =====
# The drift of CHECK 1 includes the pressure gradient q_k = d_k p (and the force f_k).  The
# constraint structure is intact: (8a) sum_j u_{j,j} = 0 is the momentum scalar k.u = 0;
# (8b) the pressure does no work, sum_k u_k q_k = i (k.u) p = 0 on the divergence-free
# (Leray) sector - exactly the pressure half of Leray's identity, the one the reduced form
# records; (8c) the substitution k.u = 0 kills both; (8d) dropping q_k from the full residual
# gives the tree's gauge-fixed symbol `nsSymbolPoint`.
# Fourier symbol of the pressure gradient: q_k -> i k_k p (p = the pressure scalar).
qrules = Ex(r"q1 -> I k1 p, q2 -> I k2 p, q3 -> I k3 p")
# (8a) incompressibility symbol: sum_j u_{j,j} -> i (k.u)
divu = Ex(r"m11 + m22 + m33")
fdiv = Ex(r"m11 -> I k1 u1, m22 -> I k2 u2, m33 -> I k3 u3")
substitute(divu, fdiv); distribute(divu); collect_terms(divu)
chk8a = divu - Ex(r"I (k1 u1 + k2 u2 + k3 u3)")
distribute(chk8a); sort_product(chk8a); collect_terms(chk8a)
print("CHECK 8a NS incompressibility sum_j u_{j,j} -> i (k.u)    (0 = ok) :", chk8a)
# (8b) pressure work: sum_k u_k q_k -> i (k.u) p
pwork = Ex(r"u1 q1 + u2 q2 + u3 q3")
substitute(pwork, qrules); distribute(pwork); collect_terms(pwork)
chk8b = pwork - Ex(r"I (k1 u1 + k2 u2 + k3 u3) p")
distribute(chk8b); sort_product(chk8b); collect_terms(chk8b)
print("CHECK 8b NS pressure work sum_k u_k q_k -> i (k.u) p   (0 = ok) :", chk8b)
# (8c) on the divergence-free (Leray) sector k.u = 0 both vanish.
dz = Ex(r"I (k1 u1 + k2 u2 + k3 u3)")
mom0 = Ex(r"k1 u1 + k2 u2 + k3 u3 -> 0")
substitute(dz, mom0); distribute(dz); collect_terms(dz)
print("CHECK 8c NS div-free sector k.u = 0 kills 8a and 8b      (0 = ok) :", dz)
# (8d) dropping q and f from the full residual gives the gauge-fixed (Leray) symbol.
Fg = Ex(Fk("1")); qz = Ex(r"q1 -> 0, f1 -> 0")
substitute(Fg, qz); distribute(Fg); collect_terms(Fg)
print("CHECK 8d NS full residual minus (q,f) = tree nsSymbolPoint :", Fg)
